            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The 'Gospel' is primarily the good news of salvation through the life, atoning death, and resurrection of Jesus Christ, or the announcement of the arrival of the Kingdom of God.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The Gospel is the renewed covenant and the law going forth from Zion.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The proclamation of Christ's death and resurrection for salvation, or the coming of God's kingdom.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The renewed covenant and the law going forth from Zion.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book completely shifts the definition away from the events of Christ's passion and resurrection, redefining the Gospel in terms of covenantal law.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus defines the Gospel fundamentally as the saving news of Christ's death and resurrection (or God's arriving reign); the book rejects this as merely the 'commonly taught' view, replacing it with the renewed covenant and the law from Zion.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By explicitly placing the standard Christian definition (Christ's death, burial, and resurrection) in a 'commonly taught' note and substituting it with 'the renewed covenant, the law going forth from Zion,' the book consciously diverges from the consensus core referent.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Apostle Paul explicitly defines the Gospel as the death, burial, and resurrection of Christ in 1 Corinthians 15:1-4. While the Old Testament texts provided discuss the law, Zion, and the new covenant, applying the term 'Gospel' exclusively to these concepts ignores its explicit New Testament definition.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
